WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.420
It’s been almost a year since the world changed&nbsp;
forever, not that anyone still human had really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:11.880
been counting the days. Almost a year since&nbsp;
the sun forsook its children and changed its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.220
rays from life-giving gifts to flesh-melting&nbsp;
weapons more fitting of Hell than Heaven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.500
In the first hours of the chaos, billions&nbsp;
were changed irreparably into abominations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.680
of flesh. That day when day broke permanently&nbsp;
altered the course of history. The remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:35.100
personnel at the SCP Foundation classified&nbsp;
it SCP-001, but what good could they do now?

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.020
It was, for all intents and purposes,&nbsp;
the end of the world as we knew it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:46.560
Would anything ever be as it was before? No,&nbsp;
in all likelihood, the world as we once knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.600
it is little more than a dying memory in the&nbsp;
minds of those who even bother remembering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.640
the times before. They’ll do little to&nbsp;
help you in this terrible new reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.560
where even stepping outside can be a&nbsp;
gateway to a fate so much worse than death.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.700
Huge, fleshy monsters slither&nbsp;
through the streets, day and night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.520
ceaselessly hunting for more victims to drag&nbsp;
out into the horrors of daylight and join their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.100
ever-growing forms. When it happened, planes&nbsp;
fell out of the sky like deadly hailstones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.140
devastating the ground underneath in a volley&nbsp;
of explosions that left so much of our world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:28.020
scarred. Cars and trucks careened off the road&nbsp;
and into buildings and ditches as their drivers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.860
exposed by the windows around&nbsp;
them, took on a liquid state.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.980
The surviving humans are in hiding - Many,&nbsp;
like you or me, want to survive and be safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:43.200
for another day. Others, who lost their minds&nbsp;
during the change or perhaps were always cruel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.240
and monstrous underneath it all, lurked in the&nbsp;
darkness waiting for others they could destroy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.160
with no law or order to stop them from&nbsp;
indulging in their worst pleasures now.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:59.340
Anyone who had survived this long was smart,&nbsp;
lucky, or some cursed combination of the two.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.200
One Wanderer, named David, didn’t quite&nbsp;
know which group he’d classify himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.060
as. He wandered through what had once been&nbsp;
the streets of New York City, every inch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.860
his body covered up with a special anti-sun&nbsp;
suit of his own making. In the early days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.720
the SCP Foundation had mass-released tutorials&nbsp;
on how to build full-body coverage suits out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:25.080
of clothes and everyday household objects. This&nbsp;
had likely saved many lives - Or at least served&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.580
to extend them for a while. Sometimes,&nbsp;
that was the best you could hope for.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.380
David knew where he was going: A&nbsp;
nearby underground parking complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:39.540
completely covered up beneath the earth,&nbsp;
where no natural light would reach. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.620
people down there had constructed their own&nbsp;
generators to have working electricity for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:49.020
emergencies and blocked off any stairs or&nbsp;
roadways to another level. The only way up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:55.260
or down was a single elevator shaft that they&nbsp;
owned and operated. It was the best way to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.600
one of those blobby monsters from descending and&nbsp;
dragging them all up to the horrors of daylight.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.940
David found the place, deftly avoiding any of&nbsp;
the monsters still roaming the streets. He’d been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:13.380
out on a food run, collecting canned goods from&nbsp;
distant bodegas: Tuna, spam, hotdogs, soups. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:18.660
sent out four scavengers a day in hopes of keeping&nbsp;
their food supplies well-stocked, drawing straws&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:25.540
each day to decide fairly. Today, David had been&nbsp;
one of them, and it had been a good day - in so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:31.320
far as any day can be good in this world. On some&nbsp;
days, when they’d sent out four scavengers, only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:38.340
one, two, or none at all had returned. Such is the&nbsp;
way with the world of the broken sun. You quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:44.700
lost your sense of what’s “fair” if you wanted to&nbsp;
survive here. David had learned that the hard way.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:50.100
He reached the elevator, powered by the generators&nbsp;
below, and knocked the secret code onto the metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:55.920
to get the doors to open. He slipped inside, and&nbsp;
once the security camera above saw it was him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.900
it descended. Soon enough, he was with the&nbsp;
others, handing in that night’s takings so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.740
they could pool their resources and&nbsp;
the most experienced cooks among them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:09.780
could prepare tonight’s dinner. They took small&nbsp;
pleasures in life whenever they could be found.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:15.480
Later that same evening, David, and four&nbsp;
others - Gus, Addison, Charlie, and Mike - sat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.580
around a campfire, eating cooked spam out of&nbsp;
their respective cans with scavenged forks.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:25.140
They did all they could to contact the&nbsp;
world outside: Posting messages online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:31.080
coded audio over any working radio and HAM&nbsp;
frequencies, even leaving notes out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.400
open with clues to their location, where&nbsp;
the blobs - whose minds were as melted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.720
and warped as their bodies - could never&nbsp;
find them. They received correspondences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.940
from all over the new world, and with them&nbsp;
came plenty of interesting new stories to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.920
share. So that was precisely what they did&nbsp;
around the underground campfire every night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:56.460
using filters they’d stolen from above to keep&nbsp;
the air pure. They told each other stories.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:02.700
Gus had heard an interesting one. Apparently,&nbsp;
out West, there was some kind of monster - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:08.040
Sculpture, made out of concrete and rebar, that&nbsp;
only moved when you weren’t looking at it. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.960
beast stalked through the dark, seemingly&nbsp;
indestructible, and would snap the necks of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.420
anyone and everyone it managed to get its rusty&nbsp;
stretches of rebar on. In the world before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:23.220
this would have seemed impossible. But here&nbsp;
and now, they were willing to believe it.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.960
Charlie told the story of a different beast.&nbsp;
They’d heard tales of a group of people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:33.120
were holed up inside a mall in Pittsburgh,&nbsp;
trying to wait out the horrors. Nobody brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.200
up the Dawn of the Dead reference lurking&nbsp;
in there. But apparently, something else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.820
was lurking in there too. One day, everyone&nbsp;
who’d been hiding in the mall was attacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:47.100
and slaughtered by some horrifying reptile that&nbsp;
tunneled out of the ground with giant claws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:52.140
and murdered almost all of them in cold&nbsp;
blood. The one that escaped had been telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:57.060
his story to anyone who listened. Nobody knew&nbsp;
where the giant reptile would turn up next.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.680
Addison tried to introduce a note of hope&nbsp;
with her story. She’d heard that out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.800
there was some mad genius working on a cure. A man&nbsp;
who always wore a black cloak and a white mask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:13.500
like one of those old-fashioned Plague Doctors.&nbsp;
He’d come closer than anybody else to synthesizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:18.480
a cure, and he and all the others with him&nbsp;
were holed up in an SCP Foundation site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.100
accepting help from anyone who happened&nbsp;
to wander by. It would certainly be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.600
nice story if it were true. But nobody&nbsp;
could really know for sure, could they?

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.160
Mike added a note of caution with his&nbsp;
story. He’d heard of another group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.600
survivors who made their way to one of those&nbsp;
so-called SCP Foundation containment sites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:41.580
but they’d found it abandoned, devoid of&nbsp;
the many experts and saviors that the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:47.220
Foundation had promised in all of those old&nbsp;
broadcasts. Instead, they were greeted by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:53.100
nightmare. A ghoulish old man covered in black&nbsp;
slime, with a permanent sadistic grin on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:57.720
face. He could walk through walls and melt&nbsp;
off skin with his burning touch. He’s always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:03.240
out there, just waiting for more unfortunate,&nbsp;
hope-filled victims to fall into his clutches.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:08.700
That sent a shudder through them all. What&nbsp;
had happened? Of course, there was the obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:13.560
problem of the treacherous burning sun up&nbsp;
above, and its melted minions down below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:19.500
but how had the world suddenly become so beset&nbsp;
with these terrible monsters? It was like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.660
gates of hell had opened, and all manner&nbsp;
of beasts had come spilling out. All that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:29.940
the human race could do now was survive,&nbsp;
but every day, the world seemed a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:35.760
less worth surviving for. Why were they here?&nbsp;
Just to be prey for these nightmare creatures?

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:37.860
David, who had been silent up to this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:41.640
finally spoke up, asking, “Have any&nbsp;
of you heard of the Black Warrior?”

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:46.860
There was silence. None had heard of this&nbsp;
one before. They waited and listened.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.240
“He saved my life,” David went on.&nbsp;
“And I’d guess I’m not the only one.”

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:56.520
The incident, as David told it, had happened weeks&nbsp;
ago, before he’d even joined up with this current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:02.460
group. He’d lost everything and everyone, and was&nbsp;
left wandering the wastes of New York City alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:07.920
He’d been hiding in the dark of an old financial&nbsp;
building for so long, but his food supply had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.380
run completely dry. He would simply need to&nbsp;
leave - He knew that if he waited any longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:18.300
even a few more hours without food, he’d&nbsp;
pass out and die of starvation in his sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.880
or be killed by one of those things. He’d&nbsp;
need to go out in daylight and forage. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:28.800
was the only way. He’d already built a total&nbsp;
coverage suit. What did he have to lose?

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.360
That very thought would bring him closer&nbsp;
to death than he’d ever been before.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.100
David wandered out, half delirious with&nbsp;
hunger, trying to find a store that hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:43.500
been looted or burned down to nothingness.&nbsp;
His vision was hazy. He needed something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:48.720
to eat and, ideally, something to drink, too.&nbsp;
He felt happier than he’d ever remembered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.280
being when he came across an abandoned&nbsp;
Japanese grocery store and filled up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:58.260
stale rice and several bottles of Ramune&nbsp;
like some con-going teenager. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:03.780
while he may have just saved his life in one&nbsp;
way, he’d put it in grave danger in another.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:08.220
The second he stepped out of the store, one&nbsp;
of those giant, terrible flesh monsters saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.140
him. His heart almost stopped as this mountain&nbsp;
of rancid, liquefied meat slithered towards him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:19.380
at shocking speed, sprouting a series of long&nbsp;
tentacles, ready to grab him, tear off his suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:25.500
and add him to its terrible collective. Trying&nbsp;
to save his own life had seemingly killed him.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.840
Of course, David tried to run, but it was&nbsp;
already too late. He felt the tendrils grab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:34.980
him and lift him off the air, pulling him&nbsp;
towards the immense and terrible flesh of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:40.920
this rampaging superorganism. It called out&nbsp;
in so many warped voices at once, crying for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:47.340
terrible unity. David knew that in mere moments,&nbsp;
it’d tear off his suit, and suddenly, he’d be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:53.280
worse than dead. But it seemed that luck was&nbsp;
about to shine on him in the strangest of ways.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:58.740
Every single one of the flesh monster’s gibbering&nbsp;
voices became unified in a blood-curdling scream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:04.620
Something swung down and cleaved the mountain&nbsp;
in half, splitting it cleanly into two smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:09.360
blobs. The tendrils immediately retracted,&nbsp;
and David was dropped to the ground as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.320
two smaller, shrieking blobs slithered&nbsp;
off, terrified, in different directions.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:18.180
At first, David had no idea what had just&nbsp;
happened as he began picking himself up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:23.100
from the ground. Then, he saw the silhouette&nbsp;
of a mighty figure standing a few feet away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:27.840
holding a bigger sword than he’d thought&nbsp;
would be possible for a normal human to carry.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.220
But this was no normal human&nbsp;
if it was even human at all.

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:37.500
He stood seven feet tall, built like an&nbsp;
old marble statue. Every single inch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:41.340
his body was covered in obsidian&nbsp;
armor, completely protecting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:46.080
warrior underneath from the oppressive rays&nbsp;
of the sun. This was the figure that David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:50.940
and all the others who’d encountered him, had&nbsp;
taken to calling the Black Warrior. Nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:56.220
knew who he was underneath. All they knew was&nbsp;
that he wandered the new world, day and night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.220 --> 00:11:02.100
slaughtering the beasts of the sun. He feared&nbsp;
nothing, and everyone else knew to fear him.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:06.240
David was on his back, staring with&nbsp;
disbelief at the Black Warrior. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:10.260
Warrior exhaled and began to walk towards&nbsp;
him. David cowered as he approached,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:12.960
but the Warrior simply kept walking past him.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:16.740
Confused and delirious with a mix of&nbsp;
fear and relief, David called out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.540
“Wait, aren’t you going to kill me?”

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:24.180
Without breaking stride, the Black Warrior&nbsp;
replied, “Where’s the honor in stomping on ants?”

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:29.160
And with that, he was gone. He’d saved&nbsp;
David’s life and then compared its value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:33.660
to that of an ant. That was the Black&nbsp;
Warrior, it seemed. He was neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:39.060
a hero nor a villain in the new world,&nbsp;
just a man looking to pick a good fight.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:43.620
And from the casualness with which he performed&nbsp;
every action, even cleaving the abominable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:49.080
flesh monsters, it was likely he hadn’t&nbsp;
faced an opponent he considered worthy… Yet.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:53.760
Once David had told his story, there&nbsp;
was silence around the campfire. David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.600
would never know if the others believed his&nbsp;
story or whether they thought it was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:02.220
the ramblings of a terrified man who’d just&nbsp;
gotten lucky. But we know that David wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:06.720
lying about the Black Warrior. Of course,&nbsp;
that was a relatively recent name for him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:15.840
The SCP Foundation had known him mainly as&nbsp;
SCP-076-2, but to himself, he was always Able.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:20.400
It had been Able’s second resurrection into&nbsp;
the world of When Day Breaks. In his first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:25.500
he’d decided to do something bold and daring.&nbsp;
He’d stormed Site-19 at its weakest moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.680
forcing several other anomalies&nbsp;
to assist him in his bloody quest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:33.660
to strike back against the SCP Foundation.&nbsp;
He’d followed and taunted Dr. Jack Bright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:38.640
then beaten this immortal of his own creation&nbsp;
within an inch of his latest life. Bright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:43.080
however, had managed to get the jump on Able&nbsp;
by transferring his soul into the body of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:48.180
of those fleshy abominations, then using his&nbsp;
collective strength to crush Able to death.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:54.600
Of course, for Able, just like Dr. Bright, death&nbsp;
had only ever been a temporary inconvenience.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:58.860
Several months later, he’d once again&nbsp;
resurrected from his black sarcophagus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:03.660
his mind still swimming with the memories of&nbsp;
his last life. He wandered out into the now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:08.340
empty Containment Area 25b, littered with&nbsp;
the bodies of the guards and researcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:13.380
he’d killed during his last escape.&nbsp;
Pathetic. They’d posed no challenge.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:17.880
What would, however, pose a challenge&nbsp;
to him would be the sun outside. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:21.600
remembered last time that the&nbsp;
sun had somehow changed. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:27.540
anyone exposed to its rays would be melted&nbsp;
into one of those… things. Able was intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:31.380
enough to realize that this was something&nbsp;
he might not be able to regenerate from.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.140
He needed a more permanent solution.

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.980
But Able had spent his last several&nbsp;
thousand years walking this earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:41.760
coming up with extremely permanent&nbsp;
solutions to all of his problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:46.740
and this would be no exception. He would&nbsp;
need to wear armor, armor that covered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:51.060
every inch of his skin. It would be the&nbsp;
only way he could move safely out there.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:55.500
Of course, he wouldn’t be caught dead&nbsp;
in the garb of the SCP Foundation - his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:58.980
pathetic would-be jailers, who had&nbsp;
fallen in droves in the face of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:02.880
new threat. He would make his own&nbsp;
armor, the way that only he could.

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:07.380
In case anyone needs a refresher, one of&nbsp;
Able’s primary anomalous abilities - In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:12.180
addition to his ability to resurrect himself&nbsp;
and his superhuman strength, speed, durability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:17.580
and combat prowess - is his innate ability to&nbsp;
pull a limitless number of obsidian weapons out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:23.760
of miniature localized pocket dimensions.&nbsp;
Swords, axes, clubs, chainsaws, scythes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:30.180
spears, hooks, maces, flails, tridents, knives,&nbsp;
cleavers… Almost anything you can imagine. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:35.820
Able’s deadliest weapon of all was his brain.&nbsp;
And it was with that, and a combination of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:40.080
his other weapons, that Able would figure&nbsp;
the perfect way out of this situation.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:44.880
First of all, he produced a mountain of weapons&nbsp;
within the site, a huge pile that would make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:49.140
an army of medieval knights go weak at the&nbsp;
knees. But he would not use these weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:53.820
for their intended purpose. Instead, he went&nbsp;
about searching the building for materials he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:58.860
could use in his brand-new project. All he needed&nbsp;
was sufficient metal he could bend into place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:03.780
and a blast furnace - One that he would eagerly&nbsp;
use the site’s in-built nuclear warhead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:08.700
designed for the explicit purpose of killing&nbsp;
him during an escape, to instead facilitate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:13.860
his new life in this new world. What a&nbsp;
wonderful plan it was shaping up to be.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:19.380
It took him several days to achieve his goal&nbsp;
and a few humiliating failures along the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:23.700
but Able was eventually able to create&nbsp;
a sufficiently robust furnace to melt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:27.480
down the many weapons he’d pulled from&nbsp;
the ether, and begin to make something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:32.340
new out of their molten extracts. Able&nbsp;
was a holistically brilliant warrior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:37.560
and to achieve that title, one can not only&nbsp;
learn how to fight but how to maintain one’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:42.660
arsenal and how to be an adept smithy. The&nbsp;
latter skill would serve able well here.

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:47.760
He used his own molten weapons to construct&nbsp;
the perfect battle armor, armor that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:53.100
cover his body entirely and protect him from the&nbsp;
deadly sun above. He made several prototypes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:57.060
testing each one by dressing up one&nbsp;
of the many Foundation corpses in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.060 --> 00:16:01.200
and pushing it out into the sun.&nbsp;
The first few soon had liquefied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:06.120
human leaking out of its seams, so Able&nbsp;
knew he needed to iterate and improve.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:11.460
Thankfully, despite what his bloodthirsty demeanor&nbsp;
might tell you, Able could be very patient when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:16.260
he needed to be - especially when that patience&nbsp;
could lead to greater bloodshed down the line.

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:21.240
More weapons, more melting, more forging,&nbsp;
more improving. Little by little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:26.160
Able was building himself the perfect suit&nbsp;
to venture out into the sunlight and find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:31.800
new prey. New battles. New violence. He&nbsp;
couldn’t be happier with the results.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:35.880
Soon enough, Able found a variation&nbsp;
that prevented even his dead test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:41.400
subjects from melting. It was perfect. He&nbsp;
even melted down the tinted glass of some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:46.800
Foundation tactical riot helmets to give&nbsp;
his own helm a black visor, just in case.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:52.380
He had done it, and now, with his new armor&nbsp;
assembled and worn on his huge, muscular body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:57.840
he was ready to go out there and make a new name&nbsp;
for himself. Even if all those who remembered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:04.380
Able were gone, the others would remember the&nbsp;
Black Warrior forever, in reverence and fear.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:08.940
Able produced a new sword to go with&nbsp;
his new look - A massive, weighted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:13.620
double-sided beast of a blade, one that&nbsp;
almost nobody but Able would be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:20.520
carry. A sword that would be part of his legend&nbsp;
forevermore. Oh, this would be so much fun.

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:24.360
For the first time since day had&nbsp;
broken that anybody had done so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:28.260
Able stepped out into the sunlight and smiled.

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:34.200
Now go check out SCP-001 When Day&nbsp;
Breaks Dr Bright and SCP-001 When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:40.140
Day Breaks SCP-682 for more terrifying&nbsp;
tales from the world of When Day Breaks!

